<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-2-22">
  <title>Resources for 2/22</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of the <em>transitive closure</em> of a relation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given a relation, construct the transitive closure of the relation.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-transitive-closure">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Transitive Closure</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on the <em>transitive closure of a relation</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />

    <problem>
      <title>Transitive Closure</title>
      <p>
      Work the following transitive closure problems.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the transitive closure of the following relation.
            </p>
            <image width="60%">
              <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="image-prep-transitive-1">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                  <definition>trans={1:[4],2:[1,2,3],4:[2],5:[3]}</definition>
                    <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
                      <network directed="yes" graph="trans" node-fill="white" labels="yes" 
                      scale="0.8" seed="16" node-stroke="no" node-size="8">
                        <node at="1" p="(-0.9,0.5)" />
                        <node at="2" p="(0,0.5)" />
                        <node at="3" p="(0.9,0.5)" />
                        <node at="4" p="(-0.5,-0.5)" />
                        <node at="5" p="(0.5,-0.5)" />
                      </network>
                    </coordinates>
                </diagram>
              </prefigure>
            </image>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the transitive closure of
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>a_1 \rightarrow a_2 \rightarrow a_3 \rightarrow a_4 \rightarrow a_5</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>a_1 \rightarrow a_2 \rightarrow a_3 \rightarrow a_4 \rightarrow a_1</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let $R$ be the relation on <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> given by <m>aRb</m> precisely when <m>\frac{b}{a}</m> is a prime number.
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Find a few ordered pairs in <m>R^+</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Prove or disprove that <m>9R^+360</m>.  Prove or disprove that <m>9R^+100</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    (Not on quiz) Based on your experiences in (a) and/or (b), conjecture what you believe <m>R^+</m> to be.  Prove your conjecture if you dare.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      <url href="">Transitive Closure and Warshall's Algorithm</url>
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>